From 6f50c1eaaaf5da27d715cffaa8cc5a0e3d1a5f88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Beany <24502088+beanyforever@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 13:11:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A save outside a job's turn logs where it came from

SiYuan's jobs read, change and save pages and databases while they hold
flushLock, one at a time. Other writers save without it, so one can
save a copy it read before a job's save over that save, and nothing
says which writers do. This logs them, so they can join the line one at
a time.

- flushLock is a turnLock: the same mutex, whose holders count as a
  turn through an atomic count. Every holder counts, the queue's jobs,
  PerformTxSync, the commit and the writers that already take the lock;
  the flusher's tick on an empty queue takes the bare mutex and isn't a
  turn.
- filesys.WriteTree, WriteTreeIfUnchanged and av.saveAttributeView call
  the new kernel/tripwire package before they write. Inside a turn that
  costs one atomic load, with no stack walk and no allocation.
- Outside a turn, a save logs one warning per call site and process:
  "tripwire: save outside a turn: <page|database> <pkg.Func>
  <file>:<line> <- <its caller>", and each further caller of a logged
  site once, as "tripwire: another caller of a save outside a turn:
  ...". The site is the first frame outside the save functions and the
  thin wrappers that only forward to them, avSaveView among them; a go
  statement that calls a save function directly is named by that
  function. The set of sites is capped at 4,096, and the line saying so
  carries the marker.
- Not logged: the first save of a new page or database (no file, and,
  for a page, no index entry placing it elsewhere, so a stale save to a
  moved page's old path logs), the missing-parent rebuild, and
  fixTreeJSONData's write-back, which doesn't go through WriteTree.

It is a guide, not a proof: a save that lands during another
goroutine's turn, a writer that reads before its turn and saves inside
it, raw writes around the save functions, and the CLI in its own
process aren't seen. It is always on.

The patch carries 28 Go tests: the test writer's 6, which read the log
in a process of their own each and fail without it; the builder's, of
the turn count, the once-per-site set, the empty tick and two callers
of one helper; and the challenger's, of a writer behind avSaveView, a
save to a path its page has left and a go statement.
